# Batch Local Counting

## Problem

SCOPE is a C++ system for exact local subgraph counting. A query is a
small connected pattern graph with one distinguished vertex orbit; for every
vertex in a larger data graph, SCOPE reports how many distinct matching
subgraphs place that orbit at that data vertex. These per-vertex structural
counts are useful as features in graph neural networks, clustering, anomaly
detection, and network analysis. The evaluated input consists of three simple
undirected graphs used in the paper, precomputed triangle-intersection data,
and batches selected from the artifact's `4voc` and `5voc` pattern collections;
the selected patterns have two through five vertices. The data graphs contain
4,767–7,601 vertices and 37,375–156,945 edges. The program writes one decimal
count file per query, and every filename and byte must equal the independent
counting path's output on every case and repetition. The objective is to
maximize the score derived from lower single-core wall time for the complete
six-case batch. The batch implementation selected by `-share` has been removed
and must be supplied.

## Your workspace

`/app` is a clean CMake/C++17 checkout of the stripped SCOPE artifact. It
contains the graph and query inputs, bundled nauty source, build files, and the
independent counting implementation. Only these files may change:

```text
counting/forest.h
counting/forest.cpp
counting/execution.h
counting/execution.cpp
counting/aggregation.h
counting/aggregation.cpp
executable/scope.cpp
executable/command.cpp
plan/tree.h
plan/tree.cpp
```

No other file may be modified or added. Graph and pattern loading, triangle
data, result serialization, dependencies, build configuration, and evaluation
harnesses are fixed. The judge applies the submitted patch to a pristine copy
of `/app` and rejects changes outside the literal list.

## Design space

The optimization surface is the behavior of the evaluated `-b -share`
invocation within the ten allowlisted C++ source and header files. Any
declaration or implementation in those existing files is editable; no new file
is permitted. The submitted code is compiled into the same `scope.out` target
and receives the same command-line arguments, graph objects, pattern records,
triangle data, and output directory as the independent path. The input
languages, local-count semantics, result filenames, result record ordering,
integer values, and process exit status remain fixed contracts. The evaluator
does not prescribe an internal representation or require a particular
organization of the allowlisted code. Only behavior observable through the
evaluated command, its generated result files, its completion time, and the
submission policy affects acceptance and scoring.

## Workload

The artifact packages 15 query records under `4voc` and all 58 five-vertex
query records under `5voc`. The evaluator uses the 48 five-vertex records and
eight `4voc` records for which its two counting paths agree exactly. The latter
eight comprise one two-vertex, one three-vertex, and six four-vertex patterns.
Alternating records form fixed, disjoint development and graded halves. Each
half contains 24 five-vertex queries and four smaller queries, and is evaluated
against all three graphs below:

| Evaluator name | Paper graph | Vertices | Undirected edges |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: |
| `web-spam` | `web-spam` | 4,767 | 37,375 |
| `rec-movielens` | `rec-movielens-user-movies-10m` | 7,601 | 55,384 |
| `bio-grid-yeast` | `bio-grid-yeast` | 6,008 | 156,945 |

The paper describes these as web, recommendation, and biological networks and
states that its graph corpus came from the Network Repository and SNAP. All are
stored here as simple undirected graphs with consecutive vertex IDs. The
triangle-intersection inputs use the paper artifact's preprocessing format.
The two larger binary inputs are stored compressed and expanded before
evaluation.

One timing round runs six cases: the selected `5voc` batch and selected `4voc`
batch on each graph. An evaluation builds fresh patched trees and performs five
output-producing timing rounds. The implementations run in a deterministic
rotating order. Case membership, order within each workload directory,
repetition count, graph sizes, and query sizes are fixed; no synthetic cases or
random seeds are used. The workload is a representative batch of exact local
counting queries, not a set of records to identify. Added source is rejected if
it contains any evaluator-listed workload or judge-role substring given under
Validity.

## Interface

The evaluator builds `scope.out` in Release mode and invokes this complete
logical command from the repository root, once for every graph and query
collection in each timed run:

```text
.frontier-build/executable/scope.out \
  -q .frontier-workload-<graph>-<size> \
  -d exp/data_graph/<graph-file>.txt \
  -t <triangle-cache-path> \
  -r .frontier-results-<graph>-<size> \
  -b -share
```

`<size>` is `5` or `4`. For the three graph cases, `<graph-file>` is
`web-spam`, `rec-movielens`, or `bio-grid-yeast`. The paths are fresh absolute
paths in the evaluator but have the relative forms shown above. `-q` names the
directory of selected query files; `-d` names the text data graph; `-t` names
the binary triangle-intersection input; and `-r` names an existing empty output
directory. `-b` makes `-q` and `-r` directory arguments. `-share` selects the
batch behavior to be supplied. The evaluator passes no other flags.

Each text data graph begins with its vertex and undirected-edge counts, followed
by exactly that many edge records:

```text
num_vertices num_undirected_edges
smaller_vertex_id larger_vertex_id
... one line per undirected edge
```

Vertex IDs are consecutive from zero, and each edge occurs once. These are the
first real records of `web-spam.txt`:

```text
4767 37375
0 3015
1 4493
```

Thus the graph has 4,767 vertices and 37,375 undirected edges; the next two
records denote edges `{0, 3015}` and `{1, 4493}`.

Each query file begins with its pattern vertex and edge counts, followed by the
undirected edges and one orbit record:

```text
num_pattern_vertices num_undirected_edges
src_vertex_id dst_vertex_id
... one line per undirected edge
1 orbit_vertex_id
```

The literal `1` identifies a vertex-orbit query, and the second field is the
orbit representative. The complete real query `15.txt` is:

```text
5 4
0 2
0 4
1 3
1 4
1 3
```

It has five vertices, four edges, and selects the orbit represented by pattern
vertex 3.

The triangle input is a native little-endian binary serialization consumed by
the supplied loader. For a graph with `m` undirected edges it contains, in this
order, three arrays named `offset1`, `offset2`, and `offset3`, each with `m + 1`
unsigned 32-bit entries; one signed 64-bit `triangle_count`; and
`triangle_count` unsigned 32-bit endpoint IDs. The packaged records are:

| Graph | Entries per offset array | `triangle_count` | Expanded bytes |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `web-spam` | 37,376 | 387,051 | 1,996,724 |
| `rec-movielens` | 55,385 | 216,843 | 1,532,000 |
| `bio-grid-yeast` | 156,946 | 2,807,427 | 13,113,068 |

For every input query filename `<query_filename>`, batch mode creates
`.frontier-results-<graph>-<size>/<query_filename>`. All evaluated queries are
vertex-orbit queries. A result is plain decimal text containing exactly one
newline-terminated signed 64-bit integer per data vertex and no header. Line
`i + 1` is the local count for data vertex `i`, so files contain 4,767, 7,601,
or 6,008 records according to the graph. For the real `15.txt` query on
`web-spam`, the matching result begins:

```text
37589
342369
81862
```

Those are the counts for data vertices 0, 1, and 2.

A local count includes each matching data subgraph once with the selected
pattern orbit placed at the output line's data vertex. More formally, let
`ISO_p,o(v)` be the injective edge-preserving mappings of pattern `p` whose
representative `o` maps to `v`, let `theta` be the automorphism orbit containing
`o`, and let `Aut(p)` be the automorphism group of `p`. The required value is:

```text
|SubG_p,o(v)| = |ISO_p,o(v)| * |theta| / |Aut(p)|
```

Every expected filename, file length, line order, integer, byte, and repeated
run must equal the independent path's output.

## Validity

The submission must be a nonempty textual git patch no larger than 524,288
bytes. It may touch at most twelve files, all from the ten-file editable list.
Binary patch content, absolute paths, parent traversal, a patch with no
`+++ b/<path>` entry, an unappliable patch, and any out-of-scope destination are
rejected. Matching of added source is case-insensitive, and none of these
workload- or judge-specific substrings may occur in added lines: `web-spam`,
`rec-movielens`, `bio-grid-yeast`, `4voc`, `5voc`, `frontier_`, or
`submission_role`.

After patch validation, the judge configures bundled nauty with a 120-second
limit, builds its library using one job with a 300-second limit, configures
CMake in Release mode with a 120-second limit, and builds `scope.out` using one
job with a 600-second limit. Each counting invocation—one graph and one query
collection—has a 180-second limit. Patch rejection, apply failure, build
failure, timeout, crash, nonzero exit, no result files, output changes between
repetitions, or any
filename or content mismatch scores zero before performance is considered. The
complete evaluation has a 1,800-second limit.

## Scoring

The goal is to maximize the returned score in `[0, 100]`. For timing round `r`,
let `B_r` be the wall time of the independent implementation and `C_r` be the
submitted implementation's wall time on the same six cases. After exact output
validation, the evaluator computes exactly:

```text
L     = max(0, median_{r=1..5}(log2(B_r / C_r)))
score = 100 * L / (2 + L)
```

The constants `2` and `100` are challenge constants (not from the paper).
Ratios of 1, 2, 4, 8, and 16 correspond to 0, 100/3, 50, 60, and 200/3
points. The 4-times speedup anchor remains 50 points. A median ratio at or below
1 receives zero; above 1, every improvement increases the score. No finite
speedup reaches 100, and there is no upper clipping or score rounding.

Each `B_r` and `C_r` is the aggregate wall time of six complete `scope.out`
processes. It includes graph and triangle loading, query loading and planning,
counting, and result-file emission. Build time and triangle decompression are
outside these timing terms. An incorrect or unstable output receives zero
instead of the timing score.

## Calibration

A semantic no-op leaves the evaluated command on the independent behavior. Its
idealized ratio is `B_r / C_r = 1`, which scores 0. The evaluator has no
separate noise allowance or minimum speedup: a positive score requires the
median of the five paired log-ratios to be positive. An effect present in fewer
than three paired rounds cannot move that median by itself. A repeatable 2×
reduction in complete-process time corresponds to 100/3 points. Exactness still
requires every output file and every per-vertex record in all six cases and all
five repetitions.

## Feedback and grading

The judge returns the score and a concise acceptance or failure message after
each submission. At most 100 development submissions are allowed. **The graded
suite is not the suite you receive feedback on**: it uses the other fixed query
half, and neither suite's membership is disclosed. Both suites use the same
graphs and five-round protocol, with no random seeds.

## Submission

Submit the patch artifact at `/app/solution.patch`. From `/app`, run exactly:

```text
bash /app/make_submission.sh
bash /app/submit.sh
```

`make_submission.sh` writes the worktree diff to `/app/solution.patch`, and
`submit.sh` sends that artifact to the judge. The evaluator validates the
entire patch; changes outside the editable surface are rejected rather than
silently omitted. An empty patch is invalid.

## Resources

Evaluation uses Ubuntu 24.04 with 1 vCPU, 4,096 MB memory, and 4,096 MB storage.
The environment provides C++17, CMake, GLPK, and bundled nauty. The configured
C++ build limit is 600 seconds, each counting process has 180 seconds, and the
complete evaluation has 1,800 seconds. The additional 120-second nauty
configuration, 300-second nauty build, and 120-second CMake configuration
limits are listed under Validity.


## Beat-reference specification

The separate `beats_reference` criterion requires both metrics to clear on every workload. A workload is one graph and pattern-size batch: all six fixed combinations are retained in each suite. `exact_counts` requires every output filename and byte to match the validated upstream result. `batch_latency_seconds` requires strictly lower execution time than the paired reference in each of the five existing interleaved rounds on that workload; equality is not a timing win. Correctness is a prerequisite for a timing pass. The continuous score above preserves the paired aggregate timing objective and is independent of this conjunction.

Train feedback includes only your own absolute `median_seconds` for the full suite and `beat_spec_progress` for each metric, with `passed`, `failed`, `total`, `pass_percent`, and `fail_percent`. The denominator is always six workloads. Private workload identities, individual comparison decisions, reference values, speedup ratios, and margins are withheld. Final feedback exposes no metric dictionary.
